vtu-cse-s3-1bcs305-2025-m3-t3.technicalConcept:
A Singly Linked List (SLL) or Chain is a dynamic collection of self-referential structures (nodes) allocated independently in heap memory.
• Node Representation in C:
```c
struct Node {
    int data;
    struct Node *next;
};
```
• Head Pointer: Points to the first node; `NULL` indicates an empty list.
• Primary Operations:
  - Insert at beginning: Allocate node, `newNode->next = head`, `head = newNode` (Time: $O(1)$).
  - Insert at end: Traverse to tail node where `curr->next == NULL`, set `curr->next = newNode` (Time: $O(N)$).
  - Delete node: Link predecessor directly to successor (`prev->next = curr->next`), then `free(curr)` (Time: $O(N)$).

codeSnippets.code:
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

struct Node {
    int data;
    struct Node *next;
};

struct Node* insertFront(struct Node *head, int val) {
    struct Node *newNode = (struct Node*)malloc(sizeof(struct Node));
    newNode->data = val;
    newNode->next = head;
    return newNode;
}

void display(struct Node *head) {
    struct Node *temp = head;
    while (temp != NULL) {
        printf("%d -> ", temp->data);
        temp = temp->next;
    }
    printf("NULL\n");
}

int main() {
    struct Node *head = NULL;
    head = insertFront(head, 30);
    head = insertFront(head, 20);
    head = insertFront(head, 10);
    display(head); // 10 -> 20 -> 30 -> NULL
    return 0;
}

