vtu-cse-s3-1bcs305-2025-m4-t4.technicalConcept:
A standard binary tree of $N$ nodes has $2N$ link fields, of which $N + 1$ are empty `NULL` pointers (wasted memory). Threaded Binary Trees (TBT) repurpose these NULL pointers as traversal 'threads'.
• Thread Rules:
  - If `left == NULL`: Replace with thread pointing to in-order predecessor.
  - If `right == NULL`: Replace with thread pointing to in-order successor.
• Node Structure:
```c
struct TNode {
    int data;
    struct TNode *left, *right;
    int lthread; // 1 if left is thread, 0 if real child
    int rthread; // 1 if right is thread, 0 if real child
};
```
• Advantage: In-order traversal requires $O(1)$ auxiliary space without recursion or stack.

codeSnippets.code:
#include <stdio.h>

struct TNode {
    int data;
    struct TNode *left, *right;
    int lthread, rthread;
};

struct TNode* inorderSuccessor(struct TNode *ptr) {
    if (ptr->rthread == 1) return ptr->right;
    ptr = ptr->right;
    while (ptr->lthread == 0) ptr = ptr->left;
    return ptr;
}

void inorder(struct TNode *root) {
    if (!root) return;
    struct TNode *curr = root;
    while (curr->lthread == 0) curr = curr->left; // Find leftmost
